      "completion_tokens": 1193,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** At room temperature, water flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is critical for biology because it allows nutrients to be transported through the bloodstream and plants to absorb minerals from the soil.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a strong \"skin\" on its surface, which allows insects to walk on it and for water to form droplets rather than spreading out flat.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate (oceans act as heat sinks) and helps keep human bodies at a stable temperature.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the **elixir of life**. It is the primary component of all living things:\n*   **Hydration:** It makes up about 60% of the human body.\n*   **Chemical Reactions:** Most chemical reactions that keep cells alive happen in water.\n*   **Protection:** It cushions organs and lubricates joints.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule ($H_2O$) that possesses extraordinary physical and chemical properties, making it the foundation of life as we know it."
